Z||000001||JSocha||10-04-2001||12:07 AM||||Once I find a set of inexpensive or free grill's, I'm having the high school bead blast them with their glass bead sandblaster.<P>Then I'm going to powder coat the grills at our company (black) and see how they turn out.<P>Anybody got a cheap set of grill's or ones that are just sitting in a junk pile that can't be used and were going to be thrown anyways just so I can experiment with?<P>BTW, heat has to be between 350 and 400 F for roughly 25 to 30 minutes for the paint to cure.  ASKO NOBEL has powder.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GarethWright||10-04-2001||03:39 AM||||I have heard that its not a good idea to cook powder coated parts in an oven you want to cook food in too.  If I find a cheap electric stove at the flea market, I want to try to do my intake and valve covers. and perhaps other things too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||10-04-2001||08:59 AM||||You definately do NOT want to powder coat in your kitchen oven.  It will ruin it for food.  You need to get an old oven you can use outside for this.  Also, it must be an electric oven.  Eastwood Co. has home powder coating kits.||||reg||4
Z||000004||lowfierogt||10-04-2001||11:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohoho:<BR><B>Has anyone tried this?<P>What brand do you use?<P>What equipment?<P>Easy or hard?<P>Pics of before and after?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>One of my Family members that lives in Mora was telling me about a place that power coated his intake for 20$.   I would have it done there.   It's cheaper to have it done.  They get paint in big supplies, much cheaper.<BR>I had mine done in the cities for 80 for valve covers and intake.   Mora place is cheaper.    I think it would be hard just for a few parts.   If you are doing it as a business it would be worth it.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||MinnGreenGT||10-04-2001||11:34 AM||||Hoho... Kev told me the other night that Ken Anderson (MFF member in Hastings) is planning on getting a Powdercoat system this winter from The Westwood company.  If you need stuff they can probably help. Otherwise I believe Darrell Morse also does (or can get) powdercoating done.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierohoho||10-04-2001||05:32 PM||||Thanks for the input.<P>I have a name, Custom Powder Coating, of the place up here but I guess he works during the day as no one is home.<P>Maybe I'll stop by tonight when I check on, he lives about 3 miles from me. <P>Anyone interested his number is 320-679-5300 that's a Minnesota area code for those not from around here.<P>I happened to see his business card on the local autoparts counter.<P>I saw a small add about powdercoating in some car magazine and checked out their web site and it is very informative about their product and even has the how-to listed, it's at <A HREF="http://www.hotcoat.com" TARGET=_blank>www.hotcoat.com</A> <P>I mentioned this to my wife and she said,"if I get a new electric stove you can have the old electric stove for the garage so you can powdercoat stuff", when, when, when will I learn to keep my mouth shut?<P>If I get in touch with the guy up here I'll post some prices if I can.<P><BR>||||reg||1
